#!/bin/sh
# PreToolUse guard: refuse AskUserQuestion unless THIS SESSION has an interview
# window open.
#
# Prose rules did not hold. An agent mid-change, unsure, reaches for the tool
# that is right there. This makes that call fail with a message telling it what
# to do instead, which is the only version that binds.
#
# The interview exception is a self-expiring window rather than a flag, because
# a flag that must be turned off is a flag left on. `interview-window open`
# stamps a file; this allows the call only while that file is fresh. Forget to
# close it and it closes itself.
#
# The window is keyed on the session id read from the JSON on stdin. It used to
# be a single file per user, which meant one agent opening an interview granted
# every other agent on the machine permission to interrupt -- and its `close`
# revoked a window another agent was relying on. Two agents pursuing two
# different north stars is the ordinary case.
#
# Escape hatch: CLAUDE_ALLOW_INTERRUPT=1 for the case nobody predicted. Set it
# deliberately, per command -- not exported in a shell profile, which would
# disable the guard permanently and silently.

WINDOW_DIR=${CLAUDE_INTERVIEW_WINDOW_DIR:-$HOME/.claude/interview-windows}
MAX_AGE_MIN=${CLAUDE_INTERVIEW_WINDOW_MINUTES:-30}

case "${CLAUDE_ALLOW_INTERRUPT:-}" in 1|true) exit 0 ;; esac

# PreToolUse delivers its context as JSON on stdin. jq if it is here, a narrow
# sed if not -- a guard that silently stops guarding because jq is missing is
# the worst of both.
INPUT=$(cat 2>/dev/null || true)
SID=""
if [ -n "$INPUT" ]; then
  if command -v jq >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    SID=$(printf '%s' "$INPUT" | jq -r '.session_id // empty' 2>/dev/null || true)
  fi
  [ -z "$SID" ] && SID=$(printf '%s' "$INPUT" \
    | sed -n 's/.*"session_id"[[:space:]]*:[[:space:]]*"\([^"]*\)".*/\1/p' | head -1)
fi

# Fail closed. Without a session id there is no way to tell whose window an open
# one belongs to, and treating "cannot tell" as "allowed" is exactly the bug
# that per-session scoping exists to fix.
if [ -n "$SID" ]; then
  WINDOW="$WINDOW_DIR/$SID"
  if [ -f "$WINDOW" ]; then
    # find -mmin avoids stat's incompatible flags across GNU and BSD, which is
    # exactly the sort of thing that breaks on one laptop and not the next.
    if [ -n "$(find "$WINDOW" -mmin "-$MAX_AGE_MIN" 2>/dev/null)" ]; then
      exit 0
    fi
    rm -f "$WINDOW"   # stale; clean up so it cannot be mistaken for open
  fi
fi

cat >&2 <<'MSG'
Blocked: no interruptions once work has started.

AskUserQuestion stops the turn with no timeout, needs someone present at that
exact moment, and loses the question entirely if the session ends first.

Use ask-async instead. Same shape, returns immediately:

  ask-async "your question" \
    --context "what you were doing and why this came up" \
    --option "Label|why this one" \
    --option "Other|why that one" \
    --assume "what you are doing meanwhile"

Then carry on with the stated assumption. The human ticks a box when they get to
it and nothing is blocked on them.

If you cannot name an assumption, you are not asking a question -- you are
stopping. File that and stop, citing the number:

  file-issue question "..." --body "blocked because ..., tried ..., unblocks if ..."

Both commands write the question to disk BEFORE trying GitHub, so a missing gh
or a dead network cannot lose it. Exit 3 means exactly that: recorded locally,
not filed yet. Carry on with your assumption and run `questions sync` later.

If the user did start an interview with you, open this session's own window
first: interview-window open
MSG
exit 2
